KG42.register("edges-538f6e5590ef9f66119ce965",{"id":"S-c7b4dc58195c2296","source":"COMP-166b347ab5fcd2a9","type":"links","target":"COMP-b9841ac99d9d29b8","evidence_class":"S","scientific_acceptance":false,"relation_scope":"original_occurrence_pair_only","internal_to_parent":false,"paper_ids":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation"],"assertions":[{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::E::v141-c-R001-E001","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001","record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R001-E001","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"bidding.demand","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R001-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"evidence_class":"S","provenance_gate":"direct_C_edge_with_constraint_and_source_excerpt","scientific_acceptance":false,"accepted_constraint_ids":["v141-c-R001-C002"],"held_constraint_ids":[],"semantic_review_status":"supported","source":"COMP-166b347ab5fcd2a9","target":"COMP-b9841ac99d9d29b8","source_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::V::coordination.price_signal","target_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::V::bidding.demand","source_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","target_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::bidding.demand","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). | Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). | Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. | Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). | Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. | Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"active":true,"source_signature":"ded0cea912cd6bb3a23bf2919c5a32da4a81ce90c4c6892f0d5c3106b8368a47","relation_scope":"original_occurrence_pair_only","internal_to_parent":false,"source_science":{"mechanism_id":"v141-M01","source_definition":"An offered energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals offering energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. A requested energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals requesting energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. Network-charge signals are provided transparently before negotiations so participants can anticipate additional costs of using the power system. Scope: Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. Condition: When exogenous terms are used as network charges. Unit-fee signals may be adapted to grid status, including differences between day and night. Scope: Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. The system operator may update unit-fee signals based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion occurrence rate; yearly revision is an example, not a mandatory frequency. Scope: Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies. Condition: When the system operator periodically revises unit fees using historical information from the previous period. Each agent broadcasts its local residuals and tests both global primal and dual stopping criteria; the decentralized negotiation repeats until convergence. Scope: Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: At each iteration, the global stopping test follows receipt of all other agents' local residuals.","target_definition":"The trading partnership set restricts demand proposals: the bilateral quantity must be zero for a counterparty outside that set. Scope: Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: When a prospective counterparty is outside the requesting agent's trading partnership set. Current bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its requested energy quantities. Scope: Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). Condition: At each negotiation iteration. Participants update requested quantities through their own optimization (6a) and individually send the resulting trade proposals to their trading partners; the negotiation process repeats until convergence. Scope: Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: During each iteration of the negotiation process, until convergence. Requested quantities must be updated consistently with the agent's total-power bounds, with total power equal to the sum of its bilateral proposals. Scope: Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. Condition: At every negotiation iteration. Each prosumer's requested bilateral quantity must remain within that prosumer's power boundaries. Scope: Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). Condition: When the requesting agent is a prosumer. The prescribed update prohibits buying requests from generators by constraining their bilateral proposed quantities to be nonnegative. Scope: Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). Condition: When the updating agent is a generator.","constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Current bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its requested energy quantities. Scope: Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). Condition: At each negotiation iteration.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","bidding.demand"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]}],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). | Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). | Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. | Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). | Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. | Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"source_record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R001-E001","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"bidding.demand","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R001-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"paper_scope_note":"The bilateral negotiated price update and the exogenous network fee signal are separately limited in meaning; partner-set constraints, incentive intent, and ADMM computational dependencies cannot be transmitted in a mixed way."}}],"assertion_ids":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::E::v141-c-R001-E001"]});
